---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/anthropology/intermediate/human-evolution-a-timeline">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Anthropology lesson. It assumes the foundational
    <em>Culture, Kin and the Long View</em> lesson and the intermediate
    <em>The Four Fields</em> lesson, where biological anthropology appears as one of the
    four. No biology beyond everyday vocabulary is required — every date, species and
    dating method used here is built within the lesson itself.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file each species by its place in the bush" widget="sort-buckets" config={anBushBuckets} />
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line in this origin story" widget="spot-error" config={anSavannahSpot} />
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>sequence the main hominin milestones from the chimp-human split to sapiens' expansion;</li>
    <li>explain why walking preceded thinking and tools preceded big brains;</li>
    <li>describe the multi-species bush — Neanderthals, Denisovans, the "hobbit" — and the evidence for it;</li>
    <li>read the mixture record: Neanderthal and Denisovan DNA in living genomes;</li>
    <li>name the dating methods behind the timeline and their limits.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Human evolution is <strong>seven million years of experiments, of which we are the only
    survivor</strong>. Two data streams tell the story — fossils and their dates, and genomes
    (living and ancient) — and they keep rewriting each other. Scrub the timeline below while
    reading: the deepest lesson of the record is that the sequence never ran the way the
    Victorian ladder promised. Walking came before thinking, tools came before big brains,
    art came before "civilisation" by tens of millennia, and for most of the story, several
    human species shared the planet at once.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="seven million years in four acts" widget="equationStep" config={evoCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four acts and watch the reversals
    accumulate — walking before big brains, tools before the genus Homo's expansion,
    several species sharing the planet for most of the story. Notice too that each act's
    dates come from a different kind of evidence (fossils, footprints, stone tools,
    genomes): the timeline is a compilation, and the acts are where its surprises live.
  </p>

  <h2>Act one: the split, and walking before anything else</h2>
  <p>
    Around 6-7 million years ago our lineage separated from the ancestors of chimpanzees
    (the genetic molecular clock puts the split at roughly this range). The earliest
    candidates are fragmentary: <strong>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</strong> (~7 mya, Chad —
    the "Toumaï" skull, with a 2022 femur analysis arguing for bipedality, contested),
    <strong>Orrorin</strong> (~6 mya, Kenya), and the better-preserved
    <strong>Ardipithecus ramidus</strong> (~4.4 mya, Ethiopia — "Ardi", with a grasping,
    opposable big toe). The surprise of Ardi was ecological: it lived in woodland, not
    open savannah. The old <strong>savannah hypothesis</strong> — upright walking evolved
    to stride across new grasslands — has given way to a mosaic-forest picture:
    bipedality emerged in trees-adjacent habitats, probably for a bundle of reasons
    (energetically cheaper travel between food patches, carrying, better thermoregulation
    in the open, free hands). The <strong>Laetoli footprints</strong> (Tanzania, 3.66 mya)
    — two individuals strolling upright through fresh volcanic ash — prove that well
    before any brain expansion: <strong>the defining human adaptation came first, with
    chimp-sized brains doing the thinking</strong>.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="bet on what came first" widget="predict" config={anLaetoliPredict} />
  <Widget no="3" caption="put the split on the deep-time scale" widget="place" config={anHomininSplitPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> humans evolved from chimpanzees, or from monkeys. The
  split around 6-7 million years ago was a branching: one population became both lineages,
  so chimpanzees are our cousins, not our ancestors — and no living ape is "unfinished"
  humanity. Modern chimps have been evolving along their own path for exactly as long as we
  have along ours, which is why the search for origins looks for fossil populations at the
  fork, not for creatures halfway between.</p>

  <h2>Act two: the australopithecines — bipedal, small-brained, successful</h2>
  <p>
    From ~4 to ~2 mya the australopithecines dominate: <strong>Lucy</strong>
    (<em>A. afarensis</em>, 3.2 mya, Ethiopia — about 1.1 m tall, brain roughly
    400-450 cc, chimp-sized, but with fully bipedal pelvis, knee and foot);
    <em>A. africanus</em> (the Taung child, South Africa, 1924 — the find that moved the
    search for humanity's origins from Asia to Africa); the robust
    <em>Paranthropus</em> line (massive jaws, sagittal crests — chewing specialists,
    a beautiful dead end). They made and used tools: the <strong>Lomekwi</strong> tools
    (Kenya, 3.3 mya — predating the genus <em>Homo</em> by half a million years,
    attribution debated) and the classic <strong>Oldowan</strong> sharp-flake kit
    (~2.6 mya, Gona, Ethiopia). Here is the reversal that reorganises the whole story:
    stone tools and expanded meat-eating came <em>before</em> the brain expansion that
    defines our genus — high-quality diets (meat, marrow, later cooking) are among the
    leading <em>explanations</em> for the expensive brain, not its consequence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> bipedality came first — locked in at Laetoli with chimp-sized
    brains — and the australopithecines carried it for two million years while tools and
    meat-eating set up the brain's expansion. Now the genus that walks out of Africa.
  </p>

  <h2>Act three: Homo expands — out of Africa, fire, handaxes</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Homo habilis</em> (2.4-1.4 mya) bridges the gap; <strong><em>Homo erectus</em></strong>
    (~1.9 mya) is the revolution: a tall, long-legged, effectively modern body plan
    (the Turkana Boy skeleton, 1.5 mya, would pass in a crowd at distance), the first
    to leave Africa (<strong>Dmanisi</strong>, Georgia, 1.8 mya — small-brained, showing
    that migration preceded big brains too), the maker of the symmetrical
    <strong>Acheulean handaxe</strong> (~1.76 mya — the standard technology for over a
    million years, longer than any tool design in history), and the first with credible
    fire: burnt bone and ash at Wonderwerk Cave (~1 mya), with unambiguous habitual use
    by a few hundred thousand years ago. Cooking changes everything downstream — softer
    diets shrink jaws and teeth, external digestion subsidises the gut and frees
    metabolic budget for the brain. By ~700 kya, <strong><em>H. heidelbergensis</em></strong>
    populations — toolmakers, big-game hunters, probably speakers of something — stand
    at the fork from which Neanderthals, Denisovans and ourselves descend.
  </p>

  <h2>Act four: the bush — several humans at once</h2>
  <p>
    The middle Pleistocene is not a ladder but a shrub. In Europe,
    <strong>Neanderthals</strong> (<em>H. neanderthalensis</em>, ~400-40 kya) evolved
    cold-adapted builds, the largest brains in the record (more than ours, on average),
    sophisticated prepared-core (Mousterian) technology, deliberate burials, care for
    the disabled, and — per dated cave art in Iberia (~65 kya, before known sapiens
    arrived) — painting. In Asia, <strong>Denisovans</strong> were discovered not by a
    skeleton but by a genome: Svante Pääbo's lab (Nobel Prize 2022) sequenced a finger
    bone from Denisova Cave into a distinct lineage; a jaw in Tibet later matched it.
    Add <em>H. floresiensis</em> (the "hobbit": Flores, ~1.1 m tall, surviving to
    ~50 kya — island dwarfing or deep lineage, still argued), <em>H. naledi</em>
    (South Africa: small-brained, ~300-236 kya, fifteen individuals deep inside a
    cave system reached through a chute — deliberate body placement claimed in 2023,
    which would scramble the "big brain = advanced behaviour" equation if it holds),
    and <em>H. luzonensis</em> (Philippines, 2019). For most of the last million years,
    <strong>there were several kinds of humans alive at once</strong>. The "when did we
    become human?" question dissolves: different humannesses appeared at different
    times in different bodies.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> evolution runs from primitive to advanced, ending in
  us. Evolution runs from <em>ancestors to descendants</em>, branching as it goes, with
  no direction and no goal. Neanderthals were not failed prototypes — they were a
  successful cold-adapted sibling species that dominated Europe for 300,000 years;
  our lineage's uniqueness is an accident of the last few millennia, not a
  predetermined summit.</p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — the ladder myth" widget="judge" config={anLadderJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> Homo erectus globalised the body plan, fire changed the
    metabolic budgets, and by the middle Pleistocene the story is a bush, not a ladder —
    several human species alive at once, none of them a failed prototype. What happened
    when our species joined the crowd is Act five.
  </p>

  <h2>Act five: sapiens — late, African, and mixed</h2>
  <p>
    Our species appears in Africa around <strong>315 kya</strong> (Jebel Irhoud, Morocco —
    whose mix of modern faces and archaic braincases shows emergence was
    <strong>pan-African and patchy</strong>, a networked population, not one "Eve site"),
    with Omo (Ethiopia) redated to ~233 kya. Early excursions out of Africa (Misliya,
    Israel, ~180 kya) fizzled; the <strong>main expansion</strong> — the one that
    populates the planet — left roughly 70-50 kya, and every non-African genome descends
    from it. The expansion met the others, and the meeting left marks:
    <strong>non-African genomes carry about 1-2% Neanderthal DNA</strong> (slightly more
    in East Asians), Melanesians carry about 4-6% Denisovan, and some of that
    introgression is <em>functional</em> — the high-altitude adaptation gene variant
    (EPAS1) that lets Tibetans thrive at 4,000 metres came from Denisovans; immune and
    skin variants came from Neanderthals. <strong>Interbreeding was normal</strong>, the
    clean-replacement story is dead, and "modern human" identity is measurably a
    mixture. Symbolic behaviour assembles over the same window, earlier than the old
    "cognitive revolution at 40 kya" template: shell beads and engraved ochre at Blombos
    Cave (South Africa, 75-100 kya), ochre-processing kits at 100 kya, then the great
    European explosion — Chauvet's lions at ~36 kya, Lascaux at ~17 kya — plus long-distance
    trade in shell and obsidian, burial with ornaments, and music (bone flutes from
    ~42 kya). The pattern is <strong>assembly, not miracle</strong>: capacities appeared
    piecemeal across Africa, then amplified as population density and exchange networks
    grew.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="guess the Neanderthal share in your genome" widget="estimate" config={anNeanderthalEstimate} />

  <h2>The Neanderthal file: how a species got rehabilitated</h2>
  <p>
    No story shows the record's self-correction better. Discovered in 1856 and
    misread for a century as shambling brutes, Neanderthals have been sequentially
    redeemed by evidence: robust, cold-adapted bodies with brains larger on average
    than ours; <strong>Mousterian</strong> prepared-core technology requiring
    sequences of imagined steps; hafted tools, pigments, shell containers; eagle-talon
    jewellery at Krapina (~130 kya — raptor feathers and talons as ornament, the
    ornithologists' surprise); and <strong>care</strong>: Shanidar 1, a man who
    survived to old age blind in one eye, with a withered arm and a healed amputation
    — impossible without sustained help, and the biological anthropologists' line
    stands: such infirmities lived with, for decades, are a record of care.
    Deliberate burial (Shanidar, La Chapelle) launched the 1950s-70s "burial wars",
    with the pollen-at-a-grave "flower burial" claim since complicated by rodent
    activity — the honest residue is that some bodies were deliberately positioned.
    Genomics then closed the case: Neanderthal populations were small, inbred,
    structured into isolated pockets (their genomes read like a demographic squeeze),
    and they exchanged genes with us repeatedly across at least several thousand
    years of contact. The current synthesis: extinction by demographic bad luck
    plus competition with a numerically rising, more networked cousin — not
    evolutionary failure. The rehabilitation is not sentimental; it is what the
    evidence says, and it teaches the general discipline of the field: every
    "primitive" claim about ancient humans has so far decayed under better data.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="reason from the skeleton" widget="branch" config={anCareBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> sapiens emerged pan-African and patchy, expanded late, mixed
    with the others, and assembled symbolic behaviour piecemeal — while the Neanderthal
    file shows the record correcting its own prejudices. None of these dates mean anything
    without clocks, so the next section opens the instrument case.
  </p>

  <h2>How the dates are known</h2>
  <p>
    The timeline is only as good as its clocks, so know them.
    <strong>Radiometric dating</strong> underpins everything: potassium-argon and
    argon-argon methods date the volcanic layers (tuffs) that sandwich East African
    fossils (ranges in the millions of years); uranium-series dating clocks flowstone
    and cave carbonates (crucial for minimum ages of cave art — the Iberian paintings'
    ~65 kya Neanderthal attribution rests entirely on uranium-thorium dates on the
    calcite crusts above the pigment); radiocarbon works only back to ~50 kya, which
    is why "the others vanish by ~40 kya" sits at the method's edge and keeps being
    re-argued. <strong>Ancient DNA</strong> adds a second, independent clock: molecular
    divergence dates, calibrated by the fossil record, and — the newest instrument —
    <strong>stratigraphic leakage detection</strong> in sediment (Denisova Cave yielded
    Denisovan DNA from soil layers with no skeletal find). When fossils, dates and
    genomes agree, the confidence is real; when they disagree (Denisova's jaw was
    predicted by genome before its anatomy appeared), the disagreement is the
    discovery.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="pair each clock with its material and range" widget="match" config={anDatingMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> fossils are dated by radiocarbon. Radiocarbon reaches
  only to about 50,000 years and works only on once-living material — useless for a
  million-year-old Homo erectus, whose bones have long since turned to stone. The deep
  timeline is built instead from the volcanic layers around the fossils (potassium-argon
  and argon-argon methods), from uranium-series clocks on cave calcite, and from molecular
  clocks in genomes — each with its own range and errors, which is why a date is a claim
  about the rocks, not the fossil itself.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What came first, walking upright or big brains — and which evidence settles the order?</li>
    <li>Why is "humans evolved from chimpanzees" wrong?</li>
    <li>How is a million-year-old fossil dated, given radiocarbon's limits?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) walking came first: the Laetoli footprints (3.66 mya)
    show an upright stride before any brain expansion, with chimp-sized brains doing the
    thinking. (2) the split about 6-7 million years ago was a branching — one population
    became both lineages, so chimpanzees are cousins, not ancestors, and no living ape is
    unfinished humanity. (3) with the volcanic layers around it: potassium-argon and
    argon-argon methods date the tuffs that sandwich East African fossils, while
    radiocarbon reaches only about 50,000 years.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Human evolution is seven million years of experiments, of which we are the only survivor.</li>
    <li>Walking came before thinking: bipedality precedes brain expansion by millions of years.</li>
    <li>Stone tools and meat-eating preceded the genus Homo's brain expansion — diet is a leading explanation, not a consequence.</li>
    <li>Homo erectus left Africa first; the Acheulean handaxe is the longest-lived tool design in history.</li>
    <li>For most of the last million years, several human species lived at once — a bush, not a ladder.</li>
    <li>Sapiens emerged pan-African around 315 kya, expanded 70-50 kya, and interbred with the others — mixture is measurable in living genomes.</li>
    <li>Every "primitive" claim about ancient humans has decayed under better data — the record self-corrects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate <em>Archaeology: Reading Material Culture</em> lesson shows how the
    artefacts on this timeline are recovered and dated, and <em>The Four Fields</em>
    places biological anthropology back in the discipline's frame. For what happened once
    symbolic behaviour had assembled, the advanced <em>Ethnographic Methods</em> lesson
    turns to the societies that carry it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Why us?</strong> The surviving-species question has no settled answer.
    Candidates: demographic luck (we were the species with the networks — long-range
    exchange, alliance-building), ecological flexibility (omnivory, coastal dispersal
    routes), cumulative culture (teaching chains that ratchet technology), and simple
    competition-plus-climate (the cold snap around 40 kya plus our arrival). Most
    evidence now points to a combination, with cumulative social learning as the
    differentiator: Neanderthals were intelligent, but their material worlds changed
    slowly; sapiens' changed <em>generationally</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The record's asymmetries.</strong> Everything you have read is biased by
    preservation and effort: Africa's tropics destroy bone, so its gaps are sampling;
    Europe is the most-prosecuted continent archaeologically, so its record is the
    template — wrongly, for the species' story; caves preserve and open sites do not,
    skewing everything toward cave behaviour. The 21st-century corrections (Asia's
    unexpected diversity, Africa's earlier and more distributed sapiens) all came from
    redressing survey bias — a methodological lesson in itself.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Routes and rhythms of the expansion.</strong> The out-of-Africa wave
    probably ran on two corridors: a northern route through the Levant (where
    early sapiens fossils ~180-120 kya show failed sorties) and a southern coastal
    one — "beachcombing" along shorelines, shell middens marking the trail —
    reaching Australia by roughly 65-50 kya, which required open-water crossings
    and therefore planning: the first evidence of humanity organised enough to
    deliberately cross a sea. Genetic coalescence dates (a population pinch
    around 70 kya) plus the archaeological spread now fit a picture of small
    groups leap-frogging coastlines at, perhaps, a kilometre or two per year —
    the slowest possible expansion producing the fastest species-wide takeover
    in the record.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Is Sahelanthropus bipedal (the femur is argued
    both ways)? Who made Lomekwi's tools? Did H. naledi bury its dead — and can
    "burial" be defined without brains? What were the Denisovans' anatomy and range
    (one jaw, teeth, a skull fragment, and genomes — the least-anatomically-known human
    species)? And where does <em>H. erectus</em>'s end lie — the youngest claims
    (Solo River, ~117-108 kya) keep it coexisting with everything else. Seven million
    years in, the record is rich enough to be certain of its outline and honest enough
    to show its holes; scrub the timeline again and notice how many tags say
    "contested" — that is what a live science looks like.
  </p>
